Based on my analysis, this section introduces a new `Savepoint` class in both the Postgres and SQLite engines. The `name` parameter is interpolated directly into SQL via f-strings (`ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT {self.name}`, `RELEASE SAVEPOINT {self.name}`, and `SAVEPOINT {name}` in the `savepoint` method). This name is user-controllable through the public `savepoint(name=...)` and `rollback_to(savepoint_name=...)` APIs.

However, the flaw classes I'm tasked to report are strictly authorization flaws: CWE-639 (IDOR), CWE-862 (Missing Authorization), and CWE-863 (Incorrect Authorization). The SQL injection concern here (CWE-89) is a real weakness and is flagged by SAST, but it is not an authorization flaw. There is no object fetched by a user-controlled identifier without ownership verification, no protected operation lacking a role check, and no incorrect authorization comparison in this section. The code is a low-level DB engine primitive with no authentication/authorization model to apply.

Therefore, there are no candidate authorization flaws in this section.

